"body": "# Framing\n\n## Buildings and walls\n\n- New page > Building or Framed wall, or Building from room on a floor plan (a rectangular room with its doors and windows).\n- Walls are seen from outside. The front and back walls run the full width; the side walls fit between them.\n- Each building gets a framing plan, one elevation per wall, floor framing and roof framing.\n\n| Part | Choices |\n|---|---|\n| Walls | Stud length, stud size, spacing, single or double top plate |\n| Openings | Doors and windows with rough opening, sill and header (flat or doubled) |\n| Floor | Joists on skids, treated or not, decking |\n| Roof | Gable or single slope, pitch, rafters, overhangs, rafter ties |\n| Sheathing | Wall and roof sheets, roof panels down the slope |\n\n## Lumber\n\n- North American or metric lumber, set in Project properties.\n- The lumber list uses the fewest boards from the lengths turned on in the building's lumber settings.\n\n## Splicing\n\n| Run | Joint |\n|---|---|\n| Plates, rim joists, ridge boards, skids | Butt joint over a stud, joist or rafter |\n| Cap plates | At least 24\" (600 mm) from the top plate joints |\n| Rafter ties | Lapped at the middle, or butted with a scab |\n| Rafters, studs, floor joists | Never spliced |\n\n- No spliced piece is shorter than the shortest piece set for the building.\n- Splices are marked on the drawings and listed as \"part 1 of 2\" in the cut list.\n\n## Building views\n\n- A report per building with framing, finished shell and exploded framing, each seen from the four corners.\n- The 3D window switches between Shell, Framing and Exploded.\n"
